{:edb/db-before-t 2137 :edb/db-after-t 2138 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535450 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:21:05.783Z" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119558 1100 "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ if $\\,\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/0ef553621590325eecc2b429ffa09cd0cd4645a2653b3fd93db25f711676c132.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119558 1100 "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ if $\\,\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119558 1145 "We know that any vector in  $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/93ae6d0ad6dbd6036c99e5d2fcb55789fef9b2e576300f13b43ed561856fa218.png) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119558 1145 "We know that any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}$. Since we are told that $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-1)_(⏟)_(x_{1})\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + (3)_(⏟)_(x_{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119559 1100 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/d094c88349d0a2c8707fafcf1a1cd63de028d56358ba8019b0527e77091e51d5.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119559 1100 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119559 1145 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2} &= x \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-3)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} =-5; - 4x_{2} = 4\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left(-1 \\right) = -5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1 = -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119559 1145 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/14c42b3d306468a8a17f5ffaef535dd39c6d5fdb079366d9f495774b6e019815.png)\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left( -1 \\right)  = -5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx_1 = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/5e92e3cfdbf8d1c81be5b47124df9992d98835668154a50ece28cce8941860ca.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119560 1100 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/4eb4b3533453f1a2c08c98bb8e6fd63e3f93563a4a2d56ecc0c0caef225b82e3.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119560 1100 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119560 1145 "We need to find $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2} + x_{3}b_{3} &= x \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{1}{3})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 1; - 9x_{2} - x_{3} = 7; \\frac{4}{3}x_{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119560 1145 "We need to find $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=7 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/61d8401f40152a8eaeb30d3dc55d9548cac508b6239a9a70e99fbed9e271805b.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119598 1100 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/dc9eaa6ef7632676a920b5032f9af213dba581552c2337471b4657a0fc26ad2f.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119598 1100 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119598 1145 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = (-5) \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_1 + 2 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535450 false] [17592186119598 1145 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/9ed5651afa52eedb2786ed4e46875636e9263f955f9c99db563ff3ebe629275f.png)\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = (-5) \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_1 + 2 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065173/assets/5972542de3b4c7c622177994f83239fb14f4ea3242cdc164a88b102b780b7da9.png)" 13194139535450 true] [17592186119744 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349688 13194139535450 true] [17592186119744 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349757 13194139535450 true] [17592186119744 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349764 13194139535450 true] [17592186119744 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349778 13194139535450 true] [17592186119744 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349785 13194139535450 true] [17592186119745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349709 13194139535450 true] [17592186119745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349716 13194139535450 true] [17592186119745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349750 13194139535450 true] [17592186119745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349813 13194139535450 true] [17592186119746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349723 13194139535450 true] [17592186119746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349730 13194139535450 true] [17592186119746 1020 #edb/ref 17